[Unit]
Description=XYZO pressure delta - archive the mean-sea-level-pressure field of every cycle
After=network.target postgresql.service xyzo-isobars.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# The database password lives in the root-only env file, not in the
# unit. A unit file is world-readable; a committed one is a published
# password.
EnvironmentFile=/etc/xyzo/xyzo.env
ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 -m worker.pressure_delta --archive
User=asher
WorkingDirectory=/var/www/vhosts/xyzo.org

# WHAT IT DOES. The isobar worker cuts the pressure of one analysis into
# contour lines; this one archives the pressure FIELD of every cycle, so
# the CHANGE between two of them can be drawn — the Δp map, where the
# pressure rose and where it fell.
#
# WHY THE FIELD AND NOT THE LINES. A difference of two sets of contour
# lines is not a field — you cannot subtract a polyline. So the archive
# holds the field (1°, 130 KB a cycle, ~190 MB a year) and the API
# differences two of them on demand, for ANY window and ANY past cycle.
# Storing the delta per window would cost 1.5 GB a year for a field
# that is 190 MB.
#
# WHY IT BACKFILLS. NOMADS keeps about ten days of GFS analyses, and the
# 24-hour change is the window everyone asks for — so the last ten days
# are fetched once. From then on the archive answers every window from
# 24 hours up to a full year, and the longer windows light up as the
# archive fills.
#
# It is cheap when there is nothing new: the cycle is probed with a HEAD
# request and an already-archived cycle is skipped.
